  "content": {
    "id": "p6-q12-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "12",
    "prompt": "The formula for finding the number of degrees Fahrenheit ($F$) for a temperature given as a number of degrees Celsius ($C$) is $F=\\frac{9}{5}C+32$.\n\nFahrenheit temperatures are still used in the USA, but in Australia we commonly use Celsius.\n\nCalculate the Fahrenheit temperatures which people in the USA would recognise for:",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p6-q12-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "the freezing point of water, $0^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$32^{\\circ}\\mathrm{F}$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}F&=\\frac{9}{5}\\times0+32\\\\F&=0+32\\\\F&=32\\end{align*}$$\nThe temperature is $32^{\\circ}\\mathrm{F}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      },
      {
        "id": "p6-q12-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "the boiling point of water, $100^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$212^{\\circ}\\mathrm{F}$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}F&=\\frac{9}{5}\\times100+32\\\\F&=180+32\\\\F&=212\\end{align*}$$\nThe temperature is $212^{\\circ}\\mathrm{F}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      },
      {
        "id": "p6-q12-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "a nice summer temperature of $25^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$77^{\\circ}\\mathrm{F}$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}F&=\\frac{9}{5}\\times25+32\\\\F&=45+32\\\\F&=77\\end{align*}$$\nThe temperature is $77^{\\circ}\\mathrm{F}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      },
      {
